1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A postman must walk every street once and return to the depot.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Such a round is called an Euler circuit: every arc once, ending where it started.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Two rounds can use the same eight streets in different orders.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
So the question is: how many legal orderings of these arcs exist?

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
This hub with four loops has eight arcs and exactly six rounds.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Before counting, check that any round exists: in-degree must equal out-degree.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A vertex with two arcs in and one out can be entered twice and left once.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Second condition: ignoring direction, the arcs must lie in one piece.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Two separate cycles are balanced everywhere and still have no round.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Both conditions pass, so the graph is Eulerian and the count is at least one.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The tempting answer: fix the first arc, then permute the other five.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
But only three rounds exist. Most orderings are not walks.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
A permutation is legal only if each arc starts where the last one ended.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
So the count needs to know the junctions, not just the number of arcs.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The right quantity is a spanning tree, counted.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
An arborescence into w is a spanning tree whose arcs all point at w.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
On the three-vertex digraph there are four exit choices and three trees.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The fourth choice is a cycle: neither of its vertices can reach the root.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
So t_w is three, and the subtraction is the whole content of "tree".

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The same three trees, and the same t_w, whichever vertex is the root.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
The BEST theorem: ec = t_w times the product of (out-degree − 1) factorial.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
The tree says which exit is kept back; the factorial orders the rest.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
On the three-vertex digraph: t_w is three, every factor is one, so three.

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The root contributes (out-degree − 1)! too, though its tree edge is the last exit.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Hypotheses: Eulerian, and circuits counted up to rotation.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Four two-arc loops at one hub: out-degree four at the hub, one elsewhere.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Only one arborescence exists: every leaf must point at the hub.

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The hub's exits can be ordered in 3! = 6 ways, so six rounds.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The six rounds, listed: which loop is walked first, second, third.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
It is 3! and not 4!, because fixing one loop removes the rotation.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Double the arcs between two vertices: out-degrees become three, three, two.

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The tree count rises to five, because a doubled arc doubles a choice.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The product is 2! × 2! × 1! = 4.

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
So 5 × 4 = 20 rounds, and enumerating all eight arcs agrees.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Neither factor alone is the answer: five is not twenty, and four is not twenty.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
t_w is computed by a determinant, not by drawing trees.

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Build the Laplacian: in-degrees on the diagonal, minus the arc counts.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Delete the root's row and column, and take the determinant.

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
On the de Bruijn graph the reduced determinant is 2, matching the two trees.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
One, three, five, two: four graphs, four determinants.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The de Bruijn graph B(2,3): four vertices, eight arcs, one per 3-bit word.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Every vertex has two arcs in and two out, so the graph is Eulerian.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
t_w is 2 and the product is 1, so exactly two circuits.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Decoded, the two circuits are 00010111 and 00011101.

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Each string contains all eight 3-bit words exactly once.

46
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Shape one: is this digraph Eulerian? Balance, then connectivity.

47
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Shape two: how many circuits? One determinant, then the factorials.

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Shape three: how many arborescences into a vertex? That is one determinant.

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The four traps: undirected input, rotations, factorials of out-degree, balance.

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Draw it, check the balance, take one determinant, multiply, state the convention.

